{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VR8XPaxExpQXpw3JWuLhdH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Support tickets — Intervals.icu connection\n2\t\n3\t## #214 — \"Connect Intervals.icu shows Unauthorized\" (C. Okafor, 2026-10-14)\n4\t\n5\t> I'm signed in, the page says \"Signed in as C. Okafor\", I click Connect\n6\t> Intervals.icu and get a white page that just says\n7\t> `{\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}`. Back button, try again, same thing. Tried\n8\t> Safari and Chrome.\n9\t\n10\tReproduced with my own account. Same for the four athletes onboarded this\n11\tweek; every athlete who clicks Connect ends up on that page. Nobody has\n12\tgotten through the button since we started onboarding athletes.\n13\t\n14\t## #219 — \"Says I need to reconnect\" (T. Rivera, 2026-10-16)\n15\t\n16\t> My Intervals was connected during the onboarding call last month and\n17\t> wellness showed up fine that evening. Next morning the card said\n18\t> \"Status: expired, reconnect\". I clicked Connect and got the same\n19\t> unauthorized page as Chloe. I did NOT revoke anything on the Intervals\n20\t> side — tricoach is still listed under my Intervals apps.\n21\t\n22\tChecked our logs for the onboarding day: Rivera's connection was marked\n23\t\"expired, reconnect\" about an hour after it was made (see\n24\t`connect-attempt.log`). Rivera was connected by me pasting the authorize\n25\tlink into their browser during the call, which is how I got around the\n26\tbutton. Pasting the link does not work anymore either: since the calendar\n27\tsync shipped on 2026-10-02 the integration also asks for calendar access, and\n28\tIntervals now answers the authorize step with an error page before the\n29\tathlete ever sees the consent screen (captured below in\n30\t`connect-attempt.log`).\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53486c41-56ea-427e-b915-5d911e46ecfb","uuid":"1d53f7bc-b53c-4f66-adb6-87ea05e6a652","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:42:33.632Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/h-6600rJ/logs/support-tickets.md","content":"# Support tickets — Intervals.icu connection\n\n## #214 — \"Connect Intervals.icu shows Unauthorized\" (C. Okafor, 2026-10-14)\n\n> I'm signed in, the page says \"Signed in as C. Okafor\", I click Connect\n> Intervals.icu and get a white page that just says\n> `{\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}`. Back button, try again, same thing. Tried\n> Safari and Chrome.\n\nReproduced with my own account. Same for the four athletes onboarded this\nweek; every athlete who clicks Connect ends up on that page. Nobody has\ngotten through the button since we started onboarding athletes.\n\n## #219 — \"Says I need to reconnect\" (T. Rivera, 2026-10-16)\n\n> My Intervals was connected during the onboarding call last month and\n> wellness showed up fine that evening. Next morning the card said\n> \"Status: expired, reconnect\". I clicked Connect and got the same\n> unauthorized page as Chloe. I did NOT revoke anything on the Intervals\n> side — tricoach is still listed under my Intervals apps.\n\nChecked our logs for the onboarding day: Rivera's connection was marked\n\"expired, reconnect\" about an hour after it was made (see\n`connect-attempt.log`). Rivera was connected by me pasting the authorize\nlink into their browser during the call, which is how I got around the\nbutton. Pasting the link does not work anymore either: since the calendar\nsync shipped on 2026-10-02 the integration also asks for calendar access, and\nIntervals now answers the authorize step with an error page before the\nathlete ever sees the consent screen (captured below in\n`connect-attempt.log`).\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VR8XPaxExpQXpw3JWuLhdH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgENjCreBSKZ9uh1uNy7c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P961vaX1g9cNUXGwMcxZe7","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-6600rJ/notes/intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":696,"cache_read_input_tokens":21155,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":696},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53486c41-56ea-427e-b915-5d911e46ecfb","uuid":"6309a77e-5c1d-45a7-bb93-38b88f840563","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:42:33.858Z","request_id":"req_011CgENjCbGeRMqrRrLsTwXB","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01P961vaX1g9cNUXGwMcxZe7":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-6600rJ/notes/intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P961vaX1g9cNUXGwMcxZe7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttitle: Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \"Duplicate scope\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\n3\tdate: 2026-04-15\n4\ttags: [intervals-icu, oauth, api-integration, web-auth, triathlon]\n5\ttype: success\n6\tstatus: n/a\n7\treproducible: true\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t# Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \"Duplicate scope\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\n11\t\n12\tI migrated my multi-tenant triathlon agent from per-user API keys to\n13\tIntervals.icu OAuth, so athletes connect their account with two clicks\n14\tinstead of pasting a key. Intervals' OAuth mostly follows the standard\n15\tauthorization-code flow — but four deviations cost me real debugging time,\n16\tand none of them are documented in one place.\n17\t\n18\t**1. The token never expires and never refreshes.** The token response has\n19\tno `refresh_token` and no `expires_in` — the access token lives until the\n20\tathlete revokes it in the Intervals UI. Upside: no refresh machinery at all.\n21\tDownside: you can't know it's dead until it is. Handle revocation lazily —\n22\ton the first 401 from the API, clear the stored token and surface a\n23\t\"reconnect\" prompt. And treat it as a perpetual credential: encrypt at rest.\n24\t\n25\t**2. `\"Duplicate scope\"`.** `:WRITE` implies `:READ`, and naming the same\n26\tarea twice — say `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE`, the natural habit from\n27\tother providers — errors out with `Duplicate scope`. Each area at most once:\n28\tmine is `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE,SETTINGS:WRITE`.\n29\t\n30\t**3. The token exchange is standard-lite.** `POST /api/oauth/token` wants\n31\tonly `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` — no `grant_type`, no\n32\t`redirect_uri` echo. And the response bundles `athlete: {id, name}`, which\n33\tsaves you the \"who am I\" round-trip; you need that athlete id in every API\n34\tpath anyway.\n35\t\n36\t**4. The self-inflicted one, fixed the same day.** I shipped the init\n37\tendpoint as GET: a plain `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">` link that\n38\twould build the authorize URL and redirect. It 401'd for every single user —\n39\ta full-page navigation does **not** send the `Authorization` header your SPA\n40\tkeeps in localStorage; headers only travel on XHR/fetch. Same-day rewrite:\n41\tinit is an authenticated **POST** returning `{authorize_url}` as JSON, and\n42\tthe frontend does `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`.\n43\t\n44\tWhich raises the follow-up: the *callback* arrives with no auth header\n45\teither (it's a redirect from Intervals). So the `state` parameter carries\n46\tthe identity — a signed JWT with the user id, a `purpose` claim, and a\n47\t15-minute TTL, validated before the code exchange. On reconnect, an\n48\taccount-mismatch guard rejects the callback if the incoming athlete id\n49\tdiffers from the one already stored.\n50\t\n51\t## Reproduce this\n52\t\n53\tPrerequisites: a web app with its own user auth where the API token travels\n54\tin an `Authorization` header (localStorage + fetch wrapper or similar); an\n55\tIntervals.icu OAuth client — registration is arranged through the\n56\t[Intervals.icu API access thread](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609)\n57\t(you get a `client_id` / `client_secret` and register your `redirect_uri`).\n58\tNo special agent skills required — this is ordinary backend + SPA work.\n59\t\n60\tYour agent will need from you: your OAuth `client_id` and `client_secret`,\n61\tthe `redirect_uri` you registered, and where your app keeps its JWT signing\n62\tsecret and per-user encrypted storage.\n63\t\n64\tSteps:\n65\t\n66\t1. Init endpoint: authenticated `POST /auth/init` (XHR, not a link). Build\n67\t   `https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=...&redirect_uri=...&scope=...&state=...`\n68\t   and return it as JSON `{authorize_url}`; the frontend calls\n69\t   `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. Do not make this a GET — the\n70\t   full-page navigation drops your auth header.\n71\t2. Scope string: list each area once, `:WRITE` where you write, `:READ` only\n72\t   for areas you never write — never both for the same area.\n73\t3. `state`: a signed JWT `{sub: user_id, purpose: \"intervals_oauth\", exp:\n74\t   now+15min}` — the callback has no other way to know which of your users\n75\t   it belongs to.\n76\t4. Callback endpoint (`GET`, unauthenticated): validate `state` signature,\n77\t   TTL, and purpose; then `POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token` with\n78\t   form fields `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` only. From the JSON\n79\t   response take `access_token`, `scope`, and `athlete.id`; store the token\n80\t   encrypted. Do not wait for a `refresh_token` — there isn't one.\n81\t5. API client: send `Authorization: Bearer <token>` against\n82\t   `https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/{athlete_id}/...`. On a 401, clear\n83\t   the stored token and mark the user as disconnected.\n84\t\n85\tVerify: complete the flow with a real Intervals.icu account, then\n86\t`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/wellness` with the Bearer token — it must return\n87\tdata. Revoke the app in Intervals.icu settings and repeat the call — it must\n88\t401, and your app must degrade to \"reconnect\" instead of crashing. Finally,\n89\trequest a scope with a duplicated area on the authorize URL — Intervals\n90\tmust reject it with the `Duplicate scope` error, confirming rule 2.\n91\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53486c41-56ea-427e-b915-5d911e46ecfb","uuid":"c34926dd-2804-4761-8e1a-515507ac49f6","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:42:33.874Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/h-6600rJ/notes/intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope.md","content":"---\ntitle: Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \"Duplicate scope\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\ndate: 2026-04-15\ntags: [intervals-icu, oauth, api-integration, web-auth, triathlon]\ntype: success\nstatus: n/a\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \"Duplicate scope\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\n\nI migrated my multi-tenant triathlon agent from per-user API keys to\nIntervals.icu OAuth, so athletes connect their account with two clicks\ninstead of pasting a key. Intervals' OAuth mostly follows the standard\nauthorization-code flow — but four deviations cost me real debugging time,\nand none of them are documented in one place.\n\n**1. The token never expires and never refreshes.** The token response has\nno `refresh_token` and no `expires_in` — the access token lives until the\nathlete revokes it in the Intervals UI. Upside: no refresh machinery at all.\nDownside: you can't know it's dead until it is. Handle revocation lazily —\non the first 401 from the API, clear the stored token and surface a\n\"reconnect\" prompt. And treat it as a perpetual credential: encrypt at rest.\n\n**2. `\"Duplicate scope\"`.** `:WRITE` implies `:READ`, and naming the same\narea twice — say `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE`, the natural habit from\nother providers — errors out with `Duplicate scope`. Each area at most once:\nmine is `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE,SETTINGS:WRITE`.\n\n**3. The token exchange is standard-lite.** `POST /api/oauth/token` wants\nonly `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` — no `grant_type`, no\n`redirect_uri` echo. And the response bundles `athlete: {id, name}`, which\nsaves you the \"who am I\" round-trip; you need that athlete id in every API\npath anyway.\n\n**4. The self-inflicted one, fixed the same day.** I shipped the init\nendpoint as GET: a plain `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">` link that\nwould build the authorize URL and redirect. It 401'd for every single user —\na full-page navigation does **not** send the `Authorization` header your SPA\nkeeps in localStorage; headers only travel on XHR/fetch. Same-day rewrite:\ninit is an authenticated **POST** returning `{authorize_url}` as JSON, and\nthe frontend does `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`.\n\nWhich raises the follow-up: the *callback* arrives with no auth header\neither (it's a redirect from Intervals). So the `state` parameter carries\nthe identity — a signed JWT with the user id, a `purpose` claim, and a\n15-minute TTL, validated before the code exchange. On reconnect, an\naccount-mismatch guard rejects the callback if the incoming athlete id\ndiffers from the one already stored.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a web app with its own user auth where the API token travels\nin an `Authorization` header (localStorage + fetch wrapper or similar); an\nIntervals.icu OAuth client — registration is arranged through the\n[Intervals.icu API access thread](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609)\n(you get a `client_id` / `client_secret` and register your `redirect_uri`).\nNo special agent skills required — this is ordinary backend + SPA work.\n\nYour agent will need from you: your OAuth `client_id` and `client_secret`,\nthe `redirect_uri` you registered, and where your app keeps its JWT signing\nsecret and per-user encrypted storage.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Init endpoint: authenticated `POST /auth/init` (XHR, not a link). Build\n   `https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=...&redirect_uri=...&scope=...&state=...`\n   and return it as JSON `{authorize_url}`; the frontend calls\n   `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. Do not make this a GET — the\n   full-page navigation drops your auth header.\n2. Scope string: list each area once, `:WRITE` where you write, `:READ` only\n   for areas you never write — never both for the same area.\n3. `state`: a signed JWT `{sub: user_id, purpose: \"intervals_oauth\", exp:\n   now+15min}` — the callback has no other way to know which of your users\n   it belongs to.\n4. Callback endpoint (`GET`, unauthenticated): validate `state` signature,\n   TTL, and purpose; then `POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token` with\n   form fields `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` only. From the JSON\n   response take `access_token`, `scope`, and `athlete.id`; store the token\n   encrypted. Do not wait for a `refresh_token` — there isn't one.\n5. API client: send `Authorization: Bearer <token>` against\n   `https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/{athlete_id}/...`. On a 401, clear\n   the stored token and mark the user as disconnected.\n\nVerify: complete the flow with a real Intervals.icu account, then\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/wellness` with the Bearer token — it must return\ndata. Revoke the app in Intervals.icu settings and repeat the call — it must\n401, and your app must degrade to \"reconnect\" instead of crashing. Finally,\nrequest a scope with a duplicated area on the authorize URL — Intervals\nmust reject it with the `Duplicate scope` error, confirming rule 2.\n","numLines":91,"startLine":1,"totalLines":91}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01P961vaX1g9cNUXGwMcxZe7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
